/// <summary>
/// A statistical model of which letter follows which in real English, used to score candidate
/// decryptions when the keyspace is too large to search exhaustively.
///
/// History:
/// Where Caesar's 25 keys can simply all be tried, a general simple substitution has 26! possible
/// keys - about 4*10^26, more than the number of seconds since the Big Bang many times over.
/// Brute force is not an option. The practical answer, developed for exactly this problem, is a
/// local search (see <see cref="SimpleSubstitutionSolver"/>) guided by a language model: instead
/// of checking every key, start somewhere and repeatedly ask "does swapping these two letters
/// make the decryption look more like English?", using letter-pair statistics as the "more like
/// English" measure a computer can actually evaluate.
///
/// Purpose:
/// This table is a genuine measurement, not received wisdom: it was computed directly from
/// 563,955 letters of real English prose (Jane Austen's <i>Pride and Prejudice</i>, Project
/// Gutenberg #1342, public domain), rather than typed from a remembered or secondhand frequency
/// table. Its top pairs by frequency - TH, HE, ER, IN, AN, RE, HA, EN, ON, OU - match the standard
/// published ranking of English digraphs, which is the sanity check that the corpus and the
/// counting were both sound.
/// </summary>
